3:Hooks that keep a model's work traced and its context grounded in the project's decisions (ADRs 0005–0008): the edit, commit and PR gates of the trace, its forced reads, the issue kept in context, the progress-comment nudge, the band and `/trace`, the guard and the archive block. It depends on `mattpocock-skills`, which supplies the skills the work goes through.
7:Every change follows one pipeline, tied to a GitHub issue: Read → Track → Branch → Implement → Review → Update → Close (ADR 0006). The `trace` skill maps each step onto Matt's skills and Claude Code's commands; the model reaches it on its own.
19:- `git commit` whose message does not reference the linked issue (`#<n>`), read as git records it: `-m`, `--trailer`, `-F <file>`, a heredoc or here-string to `-F -`, `-C`, `--fixup`, the commit an `--amend --no-edit` rewrites, with comment lines dropped under `strip` cleanup;
20:- `gh pr create` (or `gh pr new`), and `gh pr edit` changing the body, whose body does not say `Closes #<n>` (or `Fixes`, `Resolves`, with `owner/name#<n>` or the issue's URL), read from `--body-file`, else the last `--body`, outside code and HTML comments;
25:### Forced reads
27:Before an agent may make such an edit or commit, it must have read every related doc in full, itself (ADR 0007). Which docs are related is decided mechanically, never by the agent:
30:- every active ADR: a numbered file under `docs/adr/` that is not superseded, deprecated or rejected, in the working tree or in `HEAD`. An ADR whose frontmatter has `applies-to:` (a path glob or a list of them) counts only for an edit of a matching path; a glob it cannot read counts everywhere, and so does every active ADR at a commit, since what a commit takes cannot be told from its text;
31:- every text file under `docs/` that the linked issue's body names, other than notebooks and files too large for Read;
34:The glossary and the ADRs come from `HEAD` as well as the working tree, so moving, emptying or re-marking one does not drop it; a doc that is gone from the working tree is read with `git show HEAD:<path>`. Besides `git commit`, the git commands that make or move commits (`merge`, `cherry-pick`, `revert`, `am`, `rebase`, `pull`, `commit-tree`, `update-ref`) wait for the reads on an issue's branch; stopping one (`--abort`, `--quit`, `--skip`) does not. During a rebase, the branch being rebased is the issue's branch.
36:A PostToolUse hook records what each agent read, separately for the main session and each subagent, so a parent's reads never count for its subagent. A file counts when Read has returned all its lines, in one read or in chunks, exactly as they are on disk; the same text read under another name counts too, so a renamed doc stays read. A doc the agent wrote, or edited after reading all of it, stays read. The issue counts when a `gh` command and its output contain each part's text, plain or as JSON; a comment the agent posted with `--body` counts as read. Output too long for Claude Code to show counts only for the preview the agent saw; a long issue part or doc in `HEAD` counts once saved to a file outside the repository and Read there. Any other change to a file or an issue part makes its read stale. The refusal lists what is unread or stale, and the commands to read them. Without a session id in the hook input, or when GitHub cannot say what the issue says, it refuses.
38:The records live in the temp directory under `trace-reads/`, signed for the agent they belong to, so one copied from another agent or written by hand does not count. The hook refuses edits there and Bash commands that name it. A model that set out to read the signing key could still forge a record: the forced reads are a seatbelt against drift, not a sandbox.
42:The human switches the trace off by launching Claude Code with `TRACE_BYPASS=1`. The edit tools cannot: everywhere, they are refused a change to `env`, `disableAllHooks`, `enabledPlugins` or `hooks` in any Claude Code settings file (user, `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, project, local or managed), any settings change the hook cannot read or predict, and any change to installed plugins. A plugin loaded with `--plugin-dir` is ordinary code in its own repository. Bash commands that write them are the guard's.
46:The issue is the record that survives compaction (ADR 0006). When a session starts, resumes or is compacted, and when a subagent starts, a hook tells the agent the linked issue's title, its acceptance criteria (the section under that heading, or the body when it has none), its latest progress comment (one headed "Progress", else the latest comment), and the related docs it must read, with the paths a scoped ADR covers; off an issue's branch, it names the step to go back to. The issue's own text is quoted line by line, so nothing a GitHub user wrote reads as the hook's words. Long parts are cut, with the command that prints them whole. This is orientation: the forced reads still decide what may be changed. After a compaction, the agent's reads are forgotten, since its context no longer holds them, and it reads the docs again.
54:A band above the prompt shows the trace at a glance (ADR 0005): the linked issue, the pipeline with the steps done (`✓`) and the step reached (`▸`), whether the related docs are read, and the PR with its CI state (passing, failing, pending, or none without checks). Off an issue's branch it is one line, the step the gates name. On a narrow terminal the pipeline shortens to the step reached and a count, so the docs and the PR stay in view.
56:It reads the same state the hooks enforce, through the hook script's `status` event for the main session, and keeps none of its own. A step is done when its gate is satisfied: Read when every related doc is read, Implement when a commit on the branch and not on the default branch references `#<n>` as the commit gate reads it, Review when the branch has an open PR (a closed one, or one merged into another branch while the issue stays open, closes nothing), Update when the issue's last comment is newer than the branch's last push.
58:It reads the state again when a session starts, clears, compacts or changes directory, after each turn, and after each Read, Bash, Edit, Write or MultiEdit, at most once every 5 seconds. While the PR's CI is pending it asks again every minute, idle or not. When the state cannot be read, it keeps what it showed.
85:Reading them is allowed; a settings change other than the switches can be made with the Edit tool.
87:It parses the command the way a shell does. Text that only mentions a command is allowed, such as `rg 'rm -rf'` or a commit message. Commands inside `bash -c`, `$(...)`, and the heredocs and `echo` or `printf` text a shell reads as its script are checked. It reads command text, so it is a seatbelt, not a sandbox.
